Z||000000||foxgapfiero||11-08-2001||07:35 PM||||For my trailer project I need to find out how much rear spring to cut to lower the suspension to make the wheels look correct in the wheel wells. The doner car was an 84 SE so the springs are pretty strong. If I cut one coil will it drop the height 1" or will it drop it 1 1/2"or will it drop 2". This is what I need to find out. Another idea was to take the springs to a spring shop and have them compressed by a pro. This would probably give me a much better ride for the trailer because the weight of the engine will not be there.       Thanks to all that reply.<BR>" <P>------------------<BR>Mark Hoagland<BR>86GT 3.4<BR>88GT T-Top<BR>85GT caddy v-8 next swap<BR>84SE trailer next project||||reg||1
Z||000001||lowCG||11-09-2001||08:54 AM||||Cool idea for a trailer!<BR>The springs that you need are going to have to be fairly short and pretty stiff to be able and start out their travel mid-point in the suspension.I don't know tha capacity of your trailer,but maybe just some good bumpstops would work,there are some fairly large dog chew toys(Petco)that just happen to be the size of an industrial rubber spring,might be worth a shot if the length is close.||||reg||1
